  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nClear skies dominated the region this evening and for a majority of \nthe overnight period so far, thanks to dry air and ridging spreading \ninto the Great Lakes. At the time of this publishing, lingering lake \ninduced clouds were minimal across Lake Superior and eastern Upper \nMichigan and high clouds have been observed streaming into western \nLake Superior. Given the clear and dry conditions over Upper \nMichigan, effective radiational cooling has been observed across the \nforecast area. Lowest temperatures observed so far in the interior \nwest have been in the mid 20s. Lakeshore temperatures range from the \n30s to mid 40s and east has fallen into the upper 30s. I'm expecting \ncontinued cooling through the remainder of the night, with most \ninterior locations likely falling into the 20s and lakeshore regions \nmost likely to only bottom out in the 30s. \n\nMid-level ridging is anticipated to stretch across much of central \nNorth America today. Associated ridge axis will gradually press \neastward into eastern Canada ahead of an upper level low moving \nthrough northern Manitoba Sunday, before exiting east toward \nGreenland Monday. Behind the northern Manitoba low on Monday, \nridging will return for the Canadian and US plains east of the \nRockies. At the surface, an expansive 1040-1030mb high will dominate \nmuch of eastern CONUS/Canada as it shifts east out of the Plains \ntoday into the Northeast tonight. Another high dives out of the \nCanadian Rockies Sunday night, helping to reinforce ridging overhead \ninto next week. This pattern will favor dry conditions alongside \nabove normal temperatures through at least the middle of next week. \n\nToday and Sunday, warm air advection, characterized by 850mb temps \nincreasing from near 0C today at 12z to ~14C by 12z Sunday, will \nenable 50s for highs today and 60s tomorrow. This will also be \naccompanied by a dry airmass, where daytime mixing will allow many \nspots in the interior west half to sink to 25-30% RH today and 30s \nelsewhere, and potentially widespread low to mid 30s Sunday. \nAdditionally, the strong pressure gradient associated with the \nstrong surface high to our south and then east, a 985ish mb low \nmoving through northern Manitoba, and a strong 925-850mb low level \njet will kick off gusty southwesterly winds, especially during peak \nafternoon mixing each day. Currently expecting southwesterly 30-40 \nmph gusts to be common in the Keweenaw today with 20-25 mph \nelsewhere. More widespread stronger southwest winds are anticipated \nSunday given the proximity of the low to our north. Current \nexpectations are widespread 30 mph gusts with 35-40 mph being most \nlikely in the Keweenaw, Schoolcraft and Luce counties, the \nMichigamme Highlands, near lakeshores, and northeast facing slopes. \nLatest REFS and NBM does suggest 45mph + potential (35-60%) along \nthe western lakeshores of Keweenaw County Sunday, which seems \nplausible given the stronger flow over the lake.\n\nWinds will lighten Sunday evening, and continue to remain light \nthrough at least Wednesday morning thanks to another strong surface \nhigh migrating across the northern Plains toward eastern North \nAmerica. Airmass aloft will stabilize, with 850mb temps generally \nremaining between +5 and 10C, varying by model. This should yield \ndaytime highs peaking in the mid 60s for most interior locations and \ncloser to 60F lakeside, and maybe upper 50s in the Keweenaw. \nOvernight lows most nights in the 30s should be expected inland, \nwith 40s by the lakeshores. Next best shot of rain, albeit \nprobabilities are currently low, comes late Wednesday into Thursday.",
